  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The South Sudan Covid-19 Emergency Response and Health system Preparedness project</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Maternal child health project targeting pregnant women,children,male,female,adolescent and persons with disability. This is an inclusive programme offering health support and strengthening of the health system in Upper Nile Fashoda and Manyo counties.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">South Sudan&#x2019;s health system continues to suffer the effects of decades of conflict and recurrent effects bad weather and Covid 19 pandemic has stretched it further. Structures are weakened including leadership and governance, health care financing, commodities and supplies, human resources for health, service delivery, and health information systems. Even where health facilities are available, access and utilization are also not optimal given the many demand side barriers including weak community structures, poor roads especially during rainy seasons, insecurity, poverty and negative social cultural factors. Given this scenario, South Sudan has one of the worst health indicators globally. The country has a maternal mortality ratio of 1150 per 100,000 live births, and an equally high neonatal and under five mortality rates at 38.6 and 96.2 per 1000 live births, respectively. There is low health service utilization rate at 0.60 and poor coverage of critical services such penta 3 (33%) and facility-based deliveries (19.4%).</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The package of services and scope of activities to be supported include:
1.	Covid 19 emergency response including risk communication, surveillance and contact tracing, case management and vaccine administration	
2.	Child health services: Health education; routine immunization; integrated management of neonatal and childhood illnesses (IMNCI) and rapid response and referral for complicated cases; LLITNs distribution; vitamin A supplementation; promotion of adequate infant and young child feeding behaviors; nutrition screening and referral to nutrition therapeutic programs.
3.	Maternal and neonatal health services: Health education; antenatal care, skilled delivery, postnatal care of mothers and newborns, family planning, and referral to secondary health services.
4.	Basic and comprehensive emergency obstetric and newborn care: To be delivered at the PHCC Center and hospital level.
5.	Sexual and gender-based violence services: Identification, counselling, management and proper referral for victims of SGBV, including rape victims
6.	Distribution of essential medicines and supplies procured by UNICEF
7.	Emergency preparedness and response: Building the capacity of partners to detect, assess and respond to health needs in the event of emergencies and disease outbreaks. 
8.	Disease surveillance and outbreak response: In line with the nationwide Integrated Disease Surveillance Response (IDSR) and Early Warning Alert and Response (EWARS) systems.
9.	Quality improvement and supervision: In-service training; continuous quality improvement activities; infection prevention activities; supportive supervision; and promotion of procedures for proper waste management and disposal of sharps and other waste.</narrative>
  </description>
